WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.500 position:56%
I have never seen anything like what
families are going through now.

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:07.375 position:61%
I don't know how they're going to cope.

00:00:07.375 --> 00:00:11.125 position:61%
I don't know how they're going to react
when there's no food in the fridge.

00:00:11.125 --> 00:00:15.208 position:58%
I don't know what they're going to do
when their children doesn't

00:00:15.208 --> 00:00:16.791
have breakfast going to school.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.708
We can very, very much
say that the cost of living crisis

00:00:26.708 --> 00:00:29.625 position:56%
has hit different parts
of the country very, very unequally.

00:00:29.625 --> 00:00:32.666 position:56%
Inflation has predominately affected
things which people

00:00:32.666 --> 00:00:36.333 position:61%
with lower incomes spend more on,
so that is things like energy and food.

00:00:36.333 --> 00:00:39.625 position:58%
The increased price of basic products
have made life very difficult for

00:00:39.625 --> 00:00:44.166 position:61%
the lower income bracket, or the lowest
20% to 40% of earners nationwide.

00:00:45.416 --> 00:00:46.583
This is Nick de Stacpoole.

00:00:46.583 --> 00:00:48.416 position:58%
He is a single dad of three and lives

00:00:48.416 --> 00:00:51.666 position:64%
in housing provided
by the borough of Chelsea and Kensington.

00:00:51.666 --> 00:00:56.083 position:64%
He relies entirely on Universal Credit
for his income, which is paid out monthly

00:00:56.083 --> 00:00:58.250
and provided by the government
for individuals

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:00.583
who are out of work
or living on a low income.

00:01:02.166 --> 00:01:04.500
I'm looking for part-time work.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.416
Because I take my two youngest
into primary school,

00:01:07.416 --> 00:01:11.583 position:59%
the earliest I could start
if I have a job in that area is 9:30am

00:01:11.583 --> 00:01:15.125
and then I have to finish at 2:30pm
to collect them at 3:10pm.

00:01:15.125 --> 00:01:16.708
So that is the problem.

00:01:16.708 --> 00:01:21.333 position:61%
In fact, I haven't come across any jobs
yet that are just those tight hours.

00:01:21.333 --> 00:01:24.250
I don't have any family,
so I have no one to help me.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:30.208 position:61%
And the temporary work I'm applying for
is mostly close to minimum wage,

00:01:30.208 --> 00:01:33.291 position:63%
which is less than most of the childcare
in this borough.

00:01:33.291 --> 00:01:35.833 position:63%
For around a fifth of parents in the UK,

00:01:41.208 --> 00:01:46.708 position:61%
The UK is now one of the most expensive
countries for child care in the OECD.

00:01:46.708 --> 00:01:48.958 position:58%
So for those without a decent income,

00:01:48.958 --> 00:01:52.833 position:61%
there is no chance to pay for childcare
to be able to go and work.

00:01:52.833 --> 00:01:57.875 position:56%
So the Universal Credit, because
I have three children, I get top ups

00:01:57.875 --> 00:02:01.958
so it's just over £1,000 a month,
which sounds quite a lot.

00:02:01.958 --> 00:02:09.250 position:61%
But, it soon goes
because I have the gas and electricity.

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:14.458
The gas, particularly
when it's cold, is costing a lot.

00:02:14.458 --> 00:02:16.916 position:67%
Now what I do is I turn the gas on and off.

00:02:16.916 --> 00:02:20.583 position:63%
In December, with gas
and electricity prices through the roof,

00:02:20.583 --> 00:02:21.750
over 7 million

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:24.666
were going without basics
like food and heat.

00:02:24.666 --> 00:02:28.000
And 4.7 million were
behind on their bills.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.625
Food is crazily expensive.

00:02:30.625 --> 00:02:34.583 position:63%
For instance, the other day
we hadn't had ketchup for about a month,

00:02:34.583 --> 00:02:36.666
but the children said “come on,
let's have ketchup again,

00:02:36.666 --> 00:02:38.041
it's nice with the chips.”

00:02:38.041 --> 00:02:42.000 position:67%
The cheapest ketchup I could get was £4.50.

00:02:47.833 --> 00:02:51.000
This is where Dads House
has really helped.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.041
I go to lunch there once a week,

00:02:54.041 --> 00:02:58.416
but also they have a food bank
that makes all the difference.

00:02:58.416 --> 00:02:59.166
And Billy,

00:02:59.166 --> 00:03:00.458
he was a single dad himself,

00:03:00.458 --> 00:03:03.000 position:56%
he set up this charity 15 years ago.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.375 position:56%
He really helps me
because he knows I've got three kids

00:03:05.375 --> 00:03:06.625
and I haven't got any other help.

00:03:06.625 --> 00:03:10.791
It’s a food bank.
It’s not just for single dads,

00:03:10.791 --> 00:03:12.500
it’s for anyone in the community.

00:03:15.625 --> 00:03:19.250 position:67%
So, that's probably saving me £600 a month.

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:24.833 position:66%
So that enables me to stay solvent, but
without that, well, our diet would suffer.

00:03:24.833 --> 00:03:29.458
We've got roughly between 60,000
and 70,000 families registered.

00:03:29.458 --> 00:03:34.000 position:56%
We've got between 40 and 50 families
a day coming to Dads House.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.583
The demand for the work
that we do went through the roof,

00:03:37.583 --> 00:03:40.083 position:58%
especially due to the cost of living.

00:03:40.083 --> 00:03:44.125
And it's absolutely heartbreaking
when you see a mum or a dad

00:03:44.125 --> 00:03:45.916
who is absolutely struggling

00:03:45.916 --> 00:03:49.000
when it comes to not being
able to cook for their children.

00:03:50.208 --> 00:03:51.833
of independent food banks say

00:03:51.833 --> 00:03:55.208
they saw an increase in demand
compared to last year, and

00:03:56.125 --> 00:04:01.208 position:66%
reported supporting a significant number
of people needing help for the first time.

00:04:01.208 --> 00:04:04.708
We can't plan. It's impossible

00:04:04.708 --> 00:04:10.416 position:63%
because we do not get
the financial donations that we did get.

00:04:10.416 --> 00:04:16.416
And every day is really difficult
to maintain that level.

00:04:16.416 --> 00:04:20.000
Sometimes we have
had to go into our reserves

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:25.083
to use that to buy food
and to provide small grants.

00:04:25.083 --> 00:04:30.708
We struggle and it's heartbreaking
when you can't give to anyone.

00:04:32.875 --> 00:04:35.958
So we'll go to the shops
and we'll buy food

00:04:35.958 --> 00:04:41.208 position:63%
and we'll go to the bank and take money
from the account so we can give that dad

00:04:41.208 --> 00:04:44.458 position:61%
a little bit of cash, so that he can go
and buy food

00:04:44.458 --> 00:04:47.500
and he can put £50 in the electric.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:49.166
When we can’t,

00:04:49.166 --> 00:04:50.208
I can't sleep at night.

00:04:52.875 --> 00:04:58.458
£1,254 for everything for a month.

00:04:58.458 --> 00:05:02.958 position:61%
Take off at least that £254 for travel.

00:05:02.958 --> 00:05:07.041 position:59%
I've got direct debits, which is £200.

00:05:07.041 --> 00:05:09.916
So you're left with £200 a week.

00:05:09.916 --> 00:05:13.541
So I take off
£50 a week for gas and electricity.

00:05:13.541 --> 00:05:18.375
So if you buy no clothes,
no toiletries, no medical stuff,

00:05:18.375 --> 00:05:22.208
no emergencies, no luxuries,
no birthday presents,

00:05:22.208 --> 00:05:25.333
you're left with £150 for food.

00:05:25.333 --> 00:05:30.041 position:58%
And if you don't have access
to a food bank, that has got to last.

00:05:30.041 --> 00:05:34.208 position:63%
As I said before, if it wasn't
for the help I received from Dads House,

00:05:34.208 --> 00:05:38.750
it would be extremely difficult
and their diet would be affected.

00:05:40.708 --> 00:05:45.333 position:58%
Universal Credit increased around
10% in spring to help with inflation.

00:05:45.333 --> 00:05:46.958
Though this provided some relief,

00:05:46.958 --> 00:05:48.416
the food insecurity of the past

00:05:48.416 --> 00:05:52.500 position:66%
year is why the UK's leading charities
such as the Trussell Trust are petitioning

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:56.291 position:56%
for an Essentials Guarantee,
a way to avoid the increased poverty

00:05:56.291 --> 00:06:00.583 position:61%
and help with basic needs that benefits
couldn't cover for so many months.

00:06:00.583 --> 00:06:05.125 position:69%
They say the cost of living crisis has
exposed the erosion of UK's benefits system.

00:06:05.125 --> 00:06:07.958
So I think what I'm hearing
when I'm speaking to people

00:06:07.958 --> 00:06:11.958 position:59%
is that there is support out there,
but it could be utilised a lot better.

00:06:11.958 --> 00:06:15.208
When you talk to foodbanks,
people are really, really worried.

00:06:15.208 --> 00:06:18.208 position:66%
I have asked people repeatedly, like, when
do you think it's going to get better?

00:06:18.208 --> 00:06:19.875 position:56%
And there's just this bleak silence.

00:06:19.875 --> 00:06:21.291
There’s this kind of question mark.

00:06:21.291 --> 00:06:25.916
I'm incredibly grateful for
and appreciative

00:06:25.916 --> 00:06:28.833
of what you do
get help with in this country.

00:06:28.833 --> 00:06:31.125
What kind of helps keep you going?

00:06:32.041 --> 00:06:33.875
My children.

